<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahaviracharya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Mahavira (mathematician)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahaviracharya was a Jain mathematician of the ninth century who worked at the court of the Rashtrakuta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghavarsha-i">Amoghavarsha I</a>. About 850 he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganitasarasangraha">Ganitasarasangraha</a>, a comprehensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> treatise on arithmetic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> and geometry. He gave rules for operations with zero, for fractions and for permutations and combinations, and his work was widely used in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Mahāvīra, whose title Mahaviracharya means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a> the Teacher, was possibly born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. His Gaṇita-sāra-saṅgraha, the Compendium on the gist of Mathematics, separated astrology from mathematics and is the earliest Indian text entirely devoted to mathematics. It treats the subjects that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmagupta">Brahmagupta</a> had discussed, but more clearly, and takes a highly syncopated approach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, with much of the text given to the techniques needed to solve algebraic problems. It established terms for the equilateral and isosceles triangle, the rhombus, the circle and the semicircle.</p><p>Its arithmetic uses the decimal place-value system and zero, though it wrongly states that a number divided by zero is unchanged. Mahāvīra asserted that the square root of a negative number does not exist, gave a formula approximating the area and perimeter of the ellipse, methods for squares and cube roots, and the general formula for choosing r things from n. He also stated algebraic identities, among them one for the cube of a number. The chapter on the reduction of fractions gives, in verses 55 to 98, systematic rules for writing a fraction as a sum of unit fractions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Mahāvīra is highly respected among Indian mathematicians, above all for the terms he established, and his reputation spread through southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where his books inspired later mathematicians. Pavuluri Mallana translated the compendium into Telugu as Saara Sangraha Ganitamu. One of his rules for unit fractions, applied with the smallest suitable integer each time, is identical to the greedy algorithm for Egyptian fractions, and further rules of the kind were given in the 14th century in the Gaṇita-kaumudi of Nārāyaṇa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahāvīra (mathematician)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Amoghavarsha I the Jain teachers Jinasena and Gunabhadra composed the Adipurana, and the mathematician <b>Mahaviracharya</b> wrote the Ganitasarasangraha c. 850.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.31,76.65&amp;year=864" aria-label="Mysore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6656 1705 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mahaviracharya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7106 1975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7106 1975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.31,76.65&amp;year=864">What was here in 864 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
